      "id": 1,
      "text": "Salton City was planned as a 40,000‑resident resort with 12,000 lots but never reached that scale.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salton_City%2C_California",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s grid of streets remains largely empty, a relic of a failed mid‑century marine resort vision.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Salton_City,_California",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 1970s, rising sea levels forced the abandonment of most shoreline buildings, including the marina.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salton_City%2C_California",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Salton Sea itself was accidentally created in 1905 when a Colorado River breach flooded the basin.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Salton_City,_California",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Salton City has appeared in several TV shows that highlight its ghost‑town vibe, such as Life After People and Off Limits.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
